"The Maximum Length for Ducci Sequences on Z m n when n is Even (https://arxiv.org/html/2410.18204v1)\nciteturn9549view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.18204v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 688\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15: \nL16:   1. cite5†Abstract. L17:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L18:   3. cite7†2 Background L19:   4. cite8†3 Proving the Main Theorem L20:   5. cite9†References L21: \nL22: cite10†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L23: \nL24: arXiv:2410.18204v1 [math.NT] 23 Oct 2024\nL25: # The Maximum Length for Ducci Sequences on $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ when $n$ is Even\nL26: \nL27: Mark L. Lewis Address: Department of Mathematical Sciences\nL28: Kent State University\nL29: Kent, OH 44242 Email address: lewis@math.kent.edu and Shannon M. Tefft Address: Department of Mathematical Sciences\nL30: Kent State University\nL31: Kent, OH 44242 Email address: stefft@kent.edu\nL32: \nL33: Date: September 2024\nL34: ###### Abstract.\nL35: \nL36: Let $D:\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}\\to\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ be defined so\nL37: \nL38:  | $$D(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})=(x_{1}+x_{2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,x_{2}+x_{3}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,...,x_{n}+x_{1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m).$$  |\nL39: $D$ is known as the Ducci function and for $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$, $\\{D^{\\alpha}(\\mathbf{u})\\}_{\\alpha=0}^{\\infty}$ is the Ducci sequence of $\\mathbf{u}$. Every Ducci sequence enters a cycle because $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is finite. In this paper, we aim to establish an upper bound for how long it will take for a Ducci sequence in $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ to enter its cycle when $n$ is even.\nL40: ###### Key words and phrases:\nL41: \nL42: Ducci sequence, modular arithmetic, length, period, $n$-Number Game\nL43: \nL44: ###### 1991 Mathematics Subject Classification\nL45: \nL46: 20D60, 11B83, 11B50\nL47: ## 1. Introduction\nL48: \nL49: We focus on an endomorphism, $D$, on $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ such that\nL50: \nL51:  | $$D(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})=(x_{1}+x_{2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,x_{2}+x_{3}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,...,x_{n}+x_{1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m).$$  |\nL52: \nL53: We call $D$ the Ducci function, similar to [cite11†2 , cite12†8 , cite13†11 ]. If $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$, then $\\{D^{\\alpha}(\\mathbf{u})\\}_{\\alpha=0}^{\\infty}$ is known as the Ducci sequence of $\\mathbf{u}$.\nL54: Because $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is finite, every Ducci sequence eventually enters a cycle. We have a specific name for this cycle, which we give in the following definition:\nL55: ###### Definition 1.\nL56: \nL57: The Ducci cycle of $\\mathbf{u}$ is\nL58: \nL59:  | $$\\{\\mathbf{v}\\mid\\exists\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\\cup\\{0\\},\\beta\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\\ni\\mathbf{v}=D^{\\alpha+\\beta}(\\mathbf{u})=D^{\\alpha}(\\mathbf{u})\\}$$  |\nL60: \nL61: . The length of $\\mathbf{u}$, $\\mathbf{Len(u)}$, is the smallest $\\alpha$ satisfying the equation\nL62: \nL63:  | $$\\mathbf{v}=D^{\\alpha+\\beta}(\\mathbf{u})=D^{\\alpha}(\\mathbf{u})$$  |\nL64: for some $v\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ the period of $\\mathbf{u}$, $\\mathbf{Per(u)}$, is the smallest $\\beta$ that satisfies the equation.\nL65: \nL66: To see this in action, let us look at the Ducci sequence of $(0,0,0,1)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{5}^{4}$: $(0,0,0,1),(0,0,1,1),(0,1,2,1),(1,3,3,1),(4,1,4,2),(0,0,1,1)$. From here, we can see that the Ducci cycle of $(0,0,0,1)$ is $(0,0,1,1),(0,1,2,1),(1,3,3,1),(4,1,4,2),$ $(0,0,1,1)$. We can also determine that $\\text{Len}(0,0,0,1)=1$ and $\\text{Per}(0,0,0,1)=4$.\nL67: This tuple $(0,0,0,1)$ and any tuple of the form $(0,0,...,0,1)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is important when it comes to Ducci sequences. The Ducci sequence of $(0,0,...,0,1)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is called the basic Ducci sequence of $\\mathbf{\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}}$. This definition is first used on page 302 by [cite12†8 ] and also by [cite11†2 , cite14†7 , cite13†11 ]. We also define $\\mathbf{P_{m}(n)}=\\text{Per}(0,0,...,0,1)$ and $\\mathbf{L_{m}(n)}=\\text{Len}(0,0,...,0,1)$.\nL68: Both of these notations are very similar to how Definition 5 of [cite11†2 ] define them, and for $Per(0,0,...,0,1)$, the notation is also like [cite12†8 , cite13†11 ]. Therefore, our example from the previous paragraph tells us $P_{5}(4)=4$ and $L_{5}(4)=1$. These values are significant because by Lemma 1 in [cite11†2 ], if $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$, then $\\text{Len}(\\mathbf{u})\\leq L_{m}(n)$ and $\\text{Per}(\\mathbf{u})|P_{m}(n)$.\nL69: Therefore, $P_{m}(n)$ and $L_{m}(n)$ provide a maximal value for $Per(\\mathbf{u})$ and $Len(\\mathbf{u})$ respectively for any $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$. The notation of $P_{m}(n)$ representing the maximum value for a tuple in $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is also used on page 858 of [cite15†3 ].\nL70: For the rest of this paper, we are most interested in the value of $L_{m}(n)$, particularly when $n$ is even. Our goal is to prove:\nL71: ###### Theorem 2.\nL72: \nL73: Let $n$ be even. Then\nL74: \nL75:   1. (1)\nL76: \nL77: If $gcf(n,m)=1$, then $L_{m}(n)=1$.\nL78: \nL79:   2. (2)\nL80: \nL81: If there exists $p$ prime, $k,n_{1},m_{1}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$, such that $n=p^{k}n_{1}$, $m=pm_{1}$, $gcf(n_{1},m_{1})=1$ and $p\\nmid n_{1},m_{1}$, then $L_{m}(n)=p^{k}$.\nL82: \nL83:   3. (3)\nL84: \nL85: If there exists $p$ prime and $k,l,n_{1},m_{1}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$ such that $n=p^{k}n_{1}$, $m=p^{l}m_{1}$, $gcf(n_{1},m_{1})=1$, and $p\\nmid n_{1},m_{1}$ then $L_{m}(n)\\leq p^{k-1}(l(p-1)+1)$.\nL86: \nL87:   4. (4)\nL88: If there exists $p_{1},p_{2},...,p_{t}$ prime where $p_{1}<p_{2}<\\cdots<p_{t}$, for $1\\leq i\\leq t$, and $n=p_{1}^{k_{1}}p_{2}^{k_{2}}\\cdots p_{t}^{k_{t}}n_{1}$, $m=p_{1}^{l_{1}}p_{2}^{l_{2}}\\cdots p_{t}^{l_{t}}m_{1}$ with $gcf(n_{1},m_{1})=1$ and $p_{i}\\nmid n_{1},m_{1}$ for every $i$, then\nL89: \nL90:  | $$L_{m}(n)=max\\{L_{p_{i}^{l_{i}}}(n)\\;|\\;1\\leq i\\leq t\\}.$$  |\nL91: \nL92: It is worth noting that in part (3) of Theorem cite16†2 , we believe\nL93: \nL94:  | $$L_{m}(n)=p^{k-1}(l(p-1)+1).$$  |\nL95: We are able to confirm this is true for all $m\\leq 50$ and even $n\\leq 20$ where $gcf(n,m)$ is a power of a prime. We would test for larger $n,m$, but our program for computing $L_{m}(n)$ requires first finding $P_{m}(n)$, which typically gets larger as $n,m$ increase. The values of $P_{m}(n)$ get too large for our Matlab program to find.\nL96: The work in this paper was done while the second author was a Ph.D. student at Kent State University under the advisement of the first author and will appear as part of the second author’s dissertation.\nL97: ## 2. Background\nL98: The Ducci function is originally defined as an endomorphism on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ or $(\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\\cup\\{0\\})^{n}$ such that $\\bar{D}(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})=(|x_{1}-x_{2}|,x_{2}-x_{3}|,....,|x_{n}-x_{1}|)$, and is the most common definition of the Ducci function. Some examples of papers that define the Ducci function this way are [cite11†2 , cite12†8 , cite17†10 , cite13†11 , cite18†14 ]. Note that if $\\bar{D}$ is defined on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$, then $\\bar{D}(\\mathbf{u})\\in(\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\\cup\\{0\\})^{n}$.\nL99: Therefore, for simplicity, we will refer to both of these cases as Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$. Other papers, including [cite19†4 , cite20†6 , cite21†15 ], use the same formula for $\\bar{D}$ but define Ducci on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. It is of course necessary that we handle the cases of Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ and on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ separately.\nL100: Ducci sequences in both of these cases also always enter a cycle. There are discussions of why this happens on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ in [cite22†5 , cite12†8 , cite13†11 , cite18†14 ] and Theorem 2 of [cite21†15 ] proves it for Ducci on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. A well known fact for Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ is proved in Lemma 3 of [cite18†14 ]: all of the entries of a tuple in a Ducci cycle belong to $\\{0,c\\}$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$.\nL101: Since $\\bar{D}(\\lambda\\mathbf{u})=\\lambda\\bar{D}(\\mathbf{u})$ for all $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$, this means that we can focus on when Ducci is defined on $\\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ where we are using our definition of $D$ given at the beginning of the paper, particularly when examining Ducci cycles.\nL102: Theorem 1 of [cite21†15 ] proves a similar finding for Ducci on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, namely that if the Ducci sequence reaches a limit point, then all of the entries of that limit point belong to $\\{0,c\\}$, where this time we have $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nL103: Because of the significance of Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{n}$ to the original Ducci case, this leads us to wonder what happens if we define Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ using our definition given at the beginning of the paper. The first paper to look at Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ is [cite23†17 ], and is also examined in [cite11†2 , cite15†3 , cite14†7 ].\nL104: There are a few classical results from the case of Ducci on $\\mathbb{Z}^{n}$ that apply to the case when $m=2$. When looking at $L_{2}(n)$ in particular, [cite12†8 ] is the first to give that $L_{2}(n)=1$ when $n$ is odd on page 303. When $n$ is even, Theorem 6 of [cite13†11 ] gives that if $n=2^{k_{1}}+2^{k_{2}}$ where $k_{1}>k_{2}\\geq 0$, then $L_{2}(n)=2^{k_{2}}$. This is then extended by Theorem 4 of [cite24†1 ] to all even $n=2^{k}n_{1}$ where $n_{1}$ is odd. Here, $L_{2}(n)=2^{k}$.\nL105: Notice that this supports part 2 of Theorem cite16†2 when $m=p=2$.\nL106: If we allow $n$ to be odd, we have a formula for $L_{m}(n)$ from Theorem 2 of [cite25†13 ]. Specifically, if $m=2^{l}m_{1}$ where $n,m_{1}$ are odd, then $L_{m}(n)=l$. For a case where $n$ is even, Theorem 2 of [cite26†12 ], proves that if $n=2^{k}$ and $m=2^{l}$, then $L_{m}(n)=2^{k-1}(l+1)$.\nL107: \nL108: Before moving on, we would also like to give a few definitions that will be useful later. If $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$, then $\\mathbf{v}$ is a predecessor to $\\mathbf{u}$ if $D(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{u}$.\nL109: We let $\\mathbf{K(\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n})}$ be the set of all tuples in a Ducci cycle. On page 6001 of [cite11†2 ], it is stated that $K(\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n})$ is a subgroup of $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$. A proof of this is provided in Theorem 1 of [cite26†12 ].\nL110: We now consider another example of a Ducci sequence and its cycle. For this, we will look at the basic Ducci sequence of $\\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{6}$. We create a transition graph that not only gives the basic Ducci sequence, but also gives all tuples whose sequence has tuples in the basic Ducci sequence. This transition graph is given in Figure cite27†1 .\nL111: \nL112: Figure 1. Transition Graph for $\\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{6}$\nL113: Notice that we can determine that $L_{2}(6)=2$ from Figure cite27†1 , which agrees with Theorem cite16†2 . We can also see that $P_{2}(6)=6$.\nL114: It is worth noting that every tuple in Figure cite27†1 that has a predecessor has exactly two, which is the same as our $m$ in this case. This is because of Theorem 4 in [cite26†12 ], which says that if $n$ is even, then every tuple that has a predecessor has exactly $m$ predecessors. This theorem also tells us that if $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ has a predecessor, call it $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$, then the remaining predecessors are of the form\nL115: \nL116:  | $$(x_{1}+z,x_{2}-z,x_{3}+z,...,x_{n}-z)$$  |\nL117: for some $z\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}$ and all tuples of this form are a predecessor to $\\mathbf{u}$.\nL118: \nL119: All of the tuples in the transition graph in Figure cite27†1 that have a predecessor, call them $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$, also satisfy the condition that $x_{1}-x_{2}+x_{3}-\\cdots-x_{n}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;2$. In fact,we have the following theorem about this:\nL120: ###### Theorem 3.\nL121: \nL122: Let $n$ be even. $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$ has a predecessor, if and only if $x_{1}-x_{2}+x_{3}-x_{4}+\\cdots+x_{n-1}-x_{n}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$\nL123: \nL124: We first note that [cite18†14 ] proves in Lemma 5 that this is true for $m=2$ and [cite11†2 ] proves it is true when $m$ is prime in Lemma 4.\nL125: ###### Proof of Theorem cite28†3 .\nL126: \nL127: $(\\Rightarrow)$ Assume $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$ has a predecessor $(y_{1},y_{2},...,y_{n})$. Then\nL128: \nL129:  | $$y_{1}+y_{2}\\equiv x_{1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL130:  | $$y_{2}+y_{3}\\equiv x_{2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL131:  | $$\\vdots$$  |\nL132:  | $$y_{n}+y_{1}\\equiv x_{n}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m.$$  |  | (2.1)\nL133: \nL134: Subtracting the second equation from the first, we get\nL135: \nL136:  | $$y_{1}-y_{3}\\equiv x_{1}-x_{2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m.$$  |\nL137: \nL138: Adding this to the third equation, we get\nL139:  | $$y_{1}+y_{4}\\equiv x_{1}-x_{2}+x_{3}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m.$$  |\nL140: \nL141: Continuing, we get\nL142: \nL143:  | $$y_{1}-y_{n-1}\\equiv x_{1}-x_{2}+\\cdots-x_{n-2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL144: \nL145: and\nL146: \nL147:  | $$y_{1}+y_{n}\\equiv x_{1}-x_{2}+\\cdots+x_{n-1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m.$$  |\nL148: \nL149: Using this and Equation (cite29†2.1 ), we see\nL150: \nL151:  | $$x_{n}\\equiv x_{1}-x_{2}+\\cdots+x_{n-1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,$$  |\nL152: \nL153: which gives\nL154: \nL155:  | $$x_{1}-x_{2}+\\cdots+x_{n-1}-x_{n}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;m,$$  |\nL156: \nL157: and $(\\Rightarrow)$ follows.\nL158: $(\\Leftarrow)$ Assume $x_{1}-x_{2}+x_{3}-x_{4}+\\cdots+x_{n-1}-x_{n}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$. It suffices to show that there exists $y_{1},y_{2},...,y_{n}$ that satisfy the equations\nL159: \nL160:  | $$y_{1}+y_{2}\\equiv x_{1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL161:  | $$y_{2}+y_{3}\\equiv x_{2}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL162:  | $$\\vdots$$  |\nL163:  | $$y_{n}+y_{1}\\equiv x_{n}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m.$$  |\nL164: Let $y_{1}=0$. If we let $y_{2}=x_{1}$, then the first equation will be satisfied. If we then let $y_{3}=x_{2}-x_{1}$, the second equation will be satisfied. If we continue this following the structure $y_{j}=x_{j-1}-x_{j-2}+\\cdots+x_{1}$ when $j$ is even and $y_{j}=x_{j-1}-x_{j-2}+\\cdots-x_{1}$ when $j$ is odd, then the first $n-1$ equations will be satisfied.\nL165: \nL166: This would result in $y_{n}=x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}+\\cdots+x_{1}$. We then see that\nL167:  | $$y_{1}+y_{n}\\equiv x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}+\\cdots+x_{1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$$  |\nL168: \nL169: By assumption, $x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}+\\cdots+x_{1}\\equiv x_{n}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$ and $y_{1}+y_{n}\\equiv x_{n}\\;\\text{mod}\\;m$ follows, and the final equation is satisfied. Therefore, $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$ has at least one predecessor, $(y_{1},y_{2},...,y_{n})$. ∎\nL170: Moving forward, if $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$, it will be useful to have a tool that gives us information about tuples in the Ducci sequence of $\\mathbf{u}$. To do this, let us first look at the first few tuples in the Ducci sequence of a tuple $(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{m}^{n}$:\nL171:  | $$(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$$  |\nL172:  | $$(x_{1}+x_{2},x_{2}+x_{3},...,x_{n}+x_{1})$$  |\nL173:  | $$(x_{1}+2x_{2}+x_{3},x_{2}+2x_{3}+x_{4},...,x_{n}+2x_{1}+x_{2}$$  |\nL174:  | $$(x_{1}+3x_{2}+3x_{3}+x_{4},x_{2}+3x_{3}+3x_{4}+x_{5},...,x_{n}+3x_{1}+3x_{2}+x_{3})$$  |\nL175:  | $$\\vdots$$  |\nL176: We can see that the coefficients on the $x_{j}$ in the first entry of each tuple in the Ducci sequence occurs in all of the entries of that tuple for some $x_{i}$. For this reason, we will let $a_{r,s}$ represent the coefficient that is on $x_{s}$ in the first entry of $D^{r}(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$. Here $r\\geq 0$ and $1\\leq s\\leq n$. The $s$ coordinate reduces modulo $n$, with the exception that we write $a_{r,n}$ instead of $a_{r,0}$.\nL177: Note that $a_{r,s}$ also appears as the coefficient on $x_{s-i+1}$ in the $i$th entry of $D^{r}(x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{n})$. Page 6 of [cite26†12 ] provides a more thorough explanation of why we can do this. We also have that\nL178:  | $$D^{r}(0,0,...,0,1)=(a_{r,n},a_{r,n-1},...,a_{r,1}).$$  |\nL179: \nL180: Additionally, by Theorem 5 of [cite26†12 ], when $r<n$, $a_{r,s}=\\displaystyle{\\binom{r}{s-1}}$. This theorem also tells us that $a_{r+t,s}=\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{t,i}a_{r,s-i+1}}$ when $t\\geq 1$ and $a_{r,s}=a_{r-1,s}+a_{r-1,s-1}$.\nL181: ## 3. Proving the Main Theorem\nL182: \nL183: Before we can prove Theorem cite16†2 , there are a few lemmas that we will need. We begin with two lemmas about particular binomial coefficients. We believe that both Lemmas cite30†4 and cite31†5 are known and have been proven before, but we are providing proofs here for completeness.\nL184: \nL185: ###### Lemma 4.\nL186: \nL187: Let $p$ be prime and $j\\leq p^{k}-1$, $k\\geq 1$. Then\nL188: \nL189:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j}\\equiv(-1)^{j}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |\nL190: ###### Proof.\nL191: \nL192: To prove this, we will do induction on $j$.\nL193: \nL194: Basis Step $\\mathbf{j=0}$: Note that this is satisfied by $\\displaystyle{\\binom{p^{k}-1}{0}}=1$.\nL195: \nL196: Inductive Step: Now assume that $\\displaystyle{\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j-1}}\\equiv(-1)^{j-1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$. Note\nL197: \nL198:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}}{j}=\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j}+\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j-1}.$$  |\nL199: \nL200: Because we proved the $j=0$ case in the basis step, we can assume $0<j\\leq p^{k}-1$, which will give us\nL201:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j}+\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j-1}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |\nL202: \nL203: Solving for $\\displaystyle{\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j}}$, we get\nL204: \nL205:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j}\\equiv-\\binom{p^{k}-1}{j-1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |\nL206: \nL207: By induction, this is equivalent to\nL208: \nL209:  | $$-(-1)^{j-1}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$$  |\nL210: \nL211: or\nL212: \nL213:  | $$(-1)^{j}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p,$$  |\nL214: \nL215: and the lemma follows.\nL216: \nL217: ∎\nL218: \nL219: The next lemma is similar to Lemma cite30†4 :\nL220: ###### Lemma 5.\nL221: \nL222: For $k>1$ and $p$ an odd prime\nL223: \nL224:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j}\\equiv\\begin{cases}(-1)^{c}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p&j=cp^{k-1}\\\\\nL225: 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p&otherwise\\\\\nL226: \\end{cases}.$$  |\nL227: ###### Proof.\nL228: \nL229: We prove this via induction on $j$.\nL230: \nL231: Basis steps $\\mathbf{j=0,1}$: These follow from\nL232: \nL233:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{0}\\equiv 1\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$$  |\nL234: \nL235: and\nL236: \nL237:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{1}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |\nL238: \nL239: Inductive step: Assume that the lemma is true for $j^{*}<j$. For $j>0$, we use the Chu-Vandermonde identity, a proof of which can be found in [cite32†16 ] to yield\nL240: \nL241:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}}{j}=\\sum_{i=0}^{j}\\binom{p^{k-1}}{i}\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j-i}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |  | (3.1)\nL242: If $j<p^{k-1}$, the sum in (cite33†3.1 ) is also equivalent to\nL243: \nL244:  | $$\\binom{p^{k-1}}{0}\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p,$$  |\nL245: \nL246: which is congruent to $0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$, so $\\displaystyle{\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j}}\\equiv 0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$.\nL247: \nL248: If $j\\geq p^{k-1}$, the sum in (cite33†3.1 ) is equivalent to\nL249: \nL250:  | $$\\binom{p^{k-1}}{0}\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j}+\\binom{p^{k-1}}{p^{k-1}}\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j-p^{k-1}}.$$  |\nL251: \nL252: This is congruent to $0\\;\\text{mod}\\;p$ by Equation (cite33†3.1 ), we have\nL253:  | $$\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j}\\equiv-\\binom{p^{k}-p^{k-1}}{j-p^{k-1}}\\;\\text{mod}\\;p.$$  |\nL254: \n-----------------------------------